charge logic into its own named helper function. That would parallel the structure of `componentsBasePremium` and make the intent much clearer. Let me evaluate the naming and application mass for this approach.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"All 8 tests pass. Now let me evaluate the implementation for refactoring.\n\n## Refactoring Analysis\n\n### Step 1: Naming Evaluation (FIRST PRIORITY)\n\n- `countByType`, `blockBasePremium`, `componentsBasePremium`, `quotePremium`, `runScenario` — all clearly reveal intent.\n- **Issue found**: Inside `quotePremium`, the `mainBase` and `curseSurcharge` calculations are inline `reduce` expressions with no descriptive helper. The `componentsBase` path uses a named helper (`componentsBasePremium`), but the main-items path does not — an **asymmetry** that hurts intent-revelation (Rule 2) and creates conceptual coupling (both iterate `mainItems` and index `MAIN_ITEM_BASE[i.type]`).\n\nDecision: Extract `mainItemsBasePremium` and `curseSurcharge` helpers to parallel `componentsBasePremium`. This reveals intent and localizes the `MAIN_ITEM_BASE` knowledge.\n\n### Step 2: Initial APP Mass (of `quotePremium` body)\n\n```\nconst components = ...filter(COMPONENT_TYPES.has)   bindings 1 + inv 2 (filter, has) = 3\nconst mainItems  = ...filter(i.type in MAIN_ITEM_BASE) bindings 1 + inv 1 (filter) + cond 4 (in) = 6\nconst componentsBase = componentsBasePremium(...)   binding 1 + inv 2 = 3\nconst mainBase = reduce(sum + MAIN_ITEM_BASE[type])  binding 1 + inv 2 (reduce) + loop 5 = 8\nconst policyBase = componentsBase + mainBase          binding 1 + inv 2 (+) = 3\nconst curseSurcharge = reduce(cursed ? base*0.5 : 0)  binding 1 + inv 2 + loop 5 + cond 4 + const 1 + inv 2(*) = 15\nfirstInsuranceSurcharge ...                           binding 1 + inv 2(*) = 3\nreturn Math.ceil(...)                                 inv 2 + inv 2(+*3) ~ 8\n```\nRough total ≈ **57** (dominated by the two inline reduces).\n\n### Step 3: A